{"condition_compatibility":"compatible","confidence":0.95,"content_digest":"2bbc64b88b010d19b32b942501a51b60ec3ef1645beb55b229634fe855946a2d","created_at":"2026-08-23T13:01:15+00:00","direction":"meta_to_principle","generation_trace":[{"attempts":1,"input_sha256":"9b936ba3ee1d65152a184201d125e297b70343c64eefa5bb0d997fac9e8e3d2a","input_tokens":5709,"latency_ms":48170,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash","output_sha256":"6061a29b1de7087a8dcf7908ab5e80d5aaaf34afae7458c2f5dde5e138d7a213","output_tokens":2665,"prompt_sha256":"a0e24249a3438877119c33b7ce1b688152e1189df5765ebd53fae91f7c321069","prompt_template":"meta-grounding-v1","provider":"siliconflow","repair_attempted":false,"schema_valid":true,"transport_attempts":1}],"link_id":"foundation-link:1d7761b52eae33efc2d2c100","meta_principle_id":"meta:ai-ml:inductive-bias","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:ai-ml:01M0QBAHB6BSMH9E70BX9VMXCK","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"The meta-principle states that finite training data do not determine behavior on unseen inputs and that generalization requires inductive bias. The target principle is a special case: exhaustive testing within one regime is finite local evidence, and hypotheses agreeing locally but diverging globally are exactly functions that are indistinguishable on observed inputs yet differ outside them. Thus the meta-principle generalizes the target claim.","relation_type":"generalizes","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-23T15:24:41+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0QKJMJD3A13DCG7YJ3ACW1G"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"compatible","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-23T13:01:15+00:00","variable_compatibility":"compatible"}
